[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: A display of various holographic trading cards, some held by hand.
AUDIO: 'What is up guys? Zach here from the Chaos Galaxy and today I\'m going to be doing a full tutorial on how to make your own holographic trading cards. Like the ones you see here. And this can work for cards fully done on the computer, cards like mine where part of the'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'template is done on the computer and the cards are hand drawn, or just fully hand drawn cards like this one here. Before I jump into it, if you want to support my trading card game the Chaos Galaxy TCG, there\'ll be a link to the'

[00:24] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: A Canon Pixma printer with its top open, showing the paper tray.
AUDIO: 'online store in the description below where you can get your hands on some packs. Really appreciate anyone supporting the game that way. But apart from that, I\'ll get straight into this tutorial. So I\'ll start by going through a list of everything you need to make your own foil holographic trading cards.'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'First thing you\'ll need is a computer. The second thing you\'ll need is a printer. Doesn\'t matter what printer as long as it prints A4 color.'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But obviously a better quality printer will mean better quality trading cards in the end result. You\'ll need some epic trading card artwork. Today I\'m going to be using the Chaos Galaxy card Tech'

[01:01] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Hands holding a metal ruler and a craft knife.
AUDIO: 'Dragon. You can either just have the artwork like this which you put on a digital template, or like one of my old trading card games, you can design the whole card by hand and use this. I\'d more recommend though to do the template on your computer. You\'ll need some foil'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'card. This can be any color like gold, silver. I like this holographic card that you can just buy from any kind of stationery store or online for about $5. You\'ll need some cutting tools either'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scissors or I prefer a ruler and a knife cuz it gives you a nice clean cut. However, if you\'re a younger viewer, always get help from your parents at this stage. Or nowadays to be honest, I just use my phone. Just upload it onto your computer, make sure you\'re in a'

[01:39] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Adobe Photoshop 2020 interface showing image files and a partially drawn card.
AUDIO: 'nice bright room with even lighting so the white background doesn\'t come out dark. So once we have the photo of our artwork here, we\'re going to go ahead and put it on the template for our trading card. I\'ve made one up on Photoshop here'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that I use for all my uh Chaos Galaxy cards. We\'ll just put the image in like this. And now, if you don\'t know how to use Photoshop or don\'t have access to it,'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then don\'t worry. There\'s plenty of free online versions of Photoshop. Uh pixlr.com is a personal favorite of mine. You just click on create new here, and then put the dimensions of the size'

[02:18] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Pixlr E photo editor interface with a blank canvas set to trading card dimensions.
AUDIO: 'of the document you\'d like. The set dimensions of a trading card are 2.5 by 3.5 in, which is 750 by 1,050 pixels. And there you go. I\'m not going to like'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'teach you guys how to use this now. I\'ve got plenty of tutorial videos on my channel. Um but, this is how you can make the background for your trading card. So, I\'ve got this image on Photoshop. I\'m going to kind of resize it and delete the'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'background and stuff, so that it fits onto my template background nicely. Play around with the brightness and stuff. And eventually, we come out with an image like this. So, if we were printing our card without foil, this is exactly'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what we\'d want it to look like. However, going to go slightly into the science of how printers work to help you understand this process. So, if any of you guys have ever refilled an ink cartridge in a printer, you\'ll know that there\'s'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'yellow, magenta, which is like red, cyan, which is kind of blue, and then black ink cartridges. There\'s no white. Printers don\'t generally print white. So, anything we print that\'s white on this card will be left uh clear or'

[03:22] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A Mac Finder window showing a preview of the "Tek Dragon" card as a JPEG.
AUDIO: 'transparent where there\'s no ink. And this is going to leave space for our foil to shine through the card uh and give us that effect that we want. So, make some areas white where you think it\'d be cool for the foil to shine'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through. So, I\'ve come up with a few options here to show you what the foil will look like. So, we\'ve got one option here with very little white, and um there won\'t be too much foil showing through the card. Got one option with a bit of white that will hopefully make'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the artwork pop out with a shiny background. And then, an option with loads of foil that\'s going to be showing through for an extra shiny card. And when I print these out at the end, if you want to go to the end of the video to kind of see the effects that these three different cards have to figure out'

[03:58] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A Mac Finder window displaying a folder structure and image files related to card designs.
AUDIO: 'how much foil you want on your card, um then go ahead and do that. And also, because we\'re printing on an A4 sheet of paper, A4 sheets generally have the space to fit nine trading cards on. So, you you\'ve got lots of room for error'

[04:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and you can test out loads of different ways of doing your foil. You\'ve got like nine different opportunities to do well. So, once you\'ve got your card templates, you\'re going to want to save them out to JPEGs or PNG files are fine as well, um like these files I have here. And then'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re going to want to go ahead and put them into a Microsoft Word document or Pages. Sorry, I\'m using Pages cuz I have a Mac. We\'ll just drag and drop the images in and then resize them so that they are that 2.5 by 3.5 in trading card'

[04:38] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Apple Pages document with three "Tek Dragon" cards arranged on a page.
AUDIO: 'size, um so it can fit in card binders or card sleeves, um or like in deck boxes with all your Pokémon cards or Yu-Gi-Oh cards or whatever. So, this is'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the sheet we\'re going to be printing. I\'m going to go ahead and fill this up with lots of other trading cards, so I don\'t waste all this paper here and the expensive acetate that we\'ll be printing them on. Okay, so before we get printing, you need to be very careful'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with this acetate. Um this is what we\'re going to be printing the card on, which is a see-through film, and then we\'ll be sticking that see-through film over our holographic cards, so the holographic bit shines through our'

[05:14] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Hands holding a Ryman OHP/Writable Ink Jet Printer Film instruction sheet and a clear acetate sheet.
AUDIO: 'transparent image. But you need to be very careful with this stuff cuz you because loading it into your printer, always read the instructions. Now, the pack I\'ve got comes with this guideline here, so follow your guideline that comes in your pack as it might be'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different to mine. But you can really only print on this stuff on one side. So, if I take the sheet here, so it\'s telling me to print on the side facing you when the rounded corner is on the left. Uh so, that\'s the'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rounded corner. So, we need to put it into our printer this way up. So, I\'m going to load the acetate into my printer. Try to make sure not to get your fingerprints on it'

[05:52] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Apple Pages document filled with nine different trading card designs.
AUDIO: 'because cuz it\'ll kind of, you know, like touching a photograph, it might smudge things. Um also got to remove this bit of film here, which I need two hands for uh because obviously we don\'t want this on our trading cards. So, I\'ve'

[06:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'filled out my A4 Word document with trading cards that we\'re going to print onto the acetate. I\'ve got some of my other favorite Chaos Galaxy cards and I\'ve removed bits so that hopefully the foil shines through and I can find the coolest effect. Uh but we\'re going to go'

[06:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'file, print. Uh it says, "So, printing page one of two." There\'s this blank sheet here. So, just going to print page one cuz we don\'t want to waste acetate on nothing. Um and then you can change the preset so'

[06:29] [scene:hardware]
VISUAL: Close-up of a printer's paper tray.
AUDIO: 'like you\'re you do fine printing rather than just draft printing and this will make your paper better, but obviously every printer is different, so I\'m not going to detail on this. And then we\'re just going to press print.'

[06:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Acetate\'s going in. And now don\'t be disappointed if you can see it coming out the printer and it doesn\'t look very clear. Obviously, you can make out the tech dragons here, but'

[06:54] [scene:result]
VISUAL: A printed acetate sheet next to a holographic sheet, showing the transparency.
AUDIO: 'they they don\'t look very bright at all, but that\'s fine because the foil shining through this acetate will make them look amazing. So, as you can see here, I\'ll put a white background on, but all the areas'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we left white on the screen uh do not show up on the printed card. So, even if you\'ve drawn your own homemade trading card on white paper, uh the white paper bits won\'t show up. The next stage is sticking our printed cards onto the foil'

[07:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sheet. Now, I\'ve got a glue stick here. However, I think double-sided tape would be better for this. And what you want to do, you have to be quite precise with your glue stick because you only want to stick down bits of card that have ink on'

[07:32] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Hands peeling a protective film from an acetate sheet.
AUDIO: 'them. So, if we take one of our tech dragon cards here and we rub glue all over the bits without printed ink on, you\'re just going to see kind of smudged glue around the back of the card. So,'

[07:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this is why I do a lot of kind of gray borders on my cards. So, this is where I\'m going to rub the glue, and I\'d recommend you guys do the same. Of it also, obviously, make sure you glue the right side. Obviously, you can see the'

[07:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'image is on both sides, but make sure to not glue the side that\'s the right way round. Glue the backwards side here. It doesn\'t matter about going over the edge on the corners of the paper because, obviously, we\'re going to be chopping'

[08:08] [scene:result]
VISUAL: A printed acetate sheet being placed over a holographic sheet, revealing the foil effect.
AUDIO: 'the borders of the acetate sheet off at the end anyway when we go to cut out our cards. It doesn\'t have to be absolutely'

[08:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'perfect, but you can kind of see I\'ve rubbed glue just on the borders of all the cards. Then, we\'re going to take our foil sheet. Make sure the cards are the right way'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up. The alignment doesn\'t have to be perfect because we\'re going to be trimming off the edges anyway. And stick this down nice and firmly. It\'s best not to use your hands. Use like a cloth or something so that'

[08:45] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Hands using a ruler and craft knife to cut out cards from the combined acetate and foil sheet.
AUDIO: 'you don\'t get your oily fingers all over the cards. But once you\'ve stuck it down nice and firm, fortunately, there\'s a few small areas where'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like down here where glue has snuck onto the border of the card for me. But that is kind of okay. But the next step is to cut these cards out, and then we\'re done. Wow, and there we go. There\'s our foil'

