AUDIO: 'I think came out really well, too. Super Flooper here from my old game Elementals looks very nice there. And we\'ve got a Pell Fam and an Omnius also looking very cool in the foil. Because these are'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'handmade as well and held together with glue, I always think it\'s nice to give them sleeves as well. If you use the dimensions that I uh talked about in the video, these should fit into standard sized trading'

[10:02] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Hands pressing down on a holographic sheet to adhere the printed acetate.
AUDIO: 'card sleeves. Cool. So, there we go. All sleeved up. You can draw your own designs on the back. Alternatively, you can print your designs on the back of the holographic'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'card. However, from my experience, printers don\'t tend to work very well with this shiny holographic foil. Also, it might be quite difficult because you can\'t see through the sheet to align like the front of your design with the'

[10:28] [scene:result]
VISUAL: Three finished "Tek Dragon" holographic cards, sleeved.
AUDIO: 'back of your design and cutting it out might kind of lead to alignment issues, but that depends on your printer at home. Um so, that\'s why I\'ve not included this in the video, but there we have it. We have the not very foiled'

[10:42] [scene:talk]
